---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { LANGUAGE_VERSION, REPO_URL, SOURCE_PUBLIC, VERSION } from '../lib/site.mjs';
---

<Base
  title="About KerfScript"
  description="Who makes KerfScript, what it is for, how mature it is, where the source lives and how to report a problem."
>
  <article class="prose narrow">
    <h1>About KerfScript</h1>
    <p>
      KerfScript is a small programming language for laser cutting. You describe flat parts in code, and it
      writes an SVG or DXF file with cut, score and engrave layers, compensated for the width of the beam
      and laid out on your sheet. Its syntax follows OpenSCAD, so people who already write parametric
      models can read it straight away.
    </p>

    <h2>Who makes it</h2>
    <p>
      KerfScript is designed and maintained by <a href="https://mylasertools.com">MyLaserTools</a>, a
      website of design tools for laser cutting and 3D printing. It started in 2026 because the generators
      on MyLaserTools needed a language that understood layers, kerf and joinery instead of solids. The same
      interpreter runs the generators on MyLaserTools, this site's playground, and the <code>kerf</code> command.
    </p>

    <h2>How mature it is</h2>
    <p>
      This is KerfScript {VERSION}, language version {LANGUAGE_VERSION}. It is young. The language may still
      change before 1.0, and the <a href="/docs/versions/">versions page</a> explains what we promise to keep
      working in the meantime. The <a href="/docs/compatibility/">laser software page</a> lists what has
      been checked in which programs and what has not been tested yet.
    </p>

    <h2>Source and licence</h2>
    <p>
      The interpreter is free software under the GNU AGPL 3.0, with an extra permission that makes your
      designs and the files they produce yours. Commercial licences are available for closed products.
      <a href="/licence/">Licence details</a>.
    </p>
    {
      SOURCE_PUBLIC ? (
        <p>
          The source, issue tracker and releases are on <a href={REPO_URL}>GitHub</a>. Install it with
          <code>npm install @mylasertools/kerfscript</code>.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          The source repository and the npm package open with the first public release. Until then the
          playground on this site runs the current version.
        </p>
      )
    }

    <h2>Reporting a problem</h2>
    <p>
      If a file does not cut the way the code says, we want to know. Send the <code>.kerf</code> file, the
      program you opened the output in, and what went wrong to <a href="mailto:julia@mylasertools.com"
        >julia@mylasertools.com</a
      >{SOURCE_PUBLIC && <>, or open an issue on <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>GitHub</a></>}. Security
      problems go to the same address; please do not post them in public.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
